  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nA very pleasant Summer day is underway as much of the area currently \nsits in the upper 70s and low 80s with minimal humidity. This has \nbeen made possible by yesterday's frontal boundary and the large \nupper low that is slowly making its way southeast across the upper \nMississippi River valley. Expect fairly breezy northerly winds for \nthe remainder of the afternoon today with mostly clear skies and \nhighs in the low to mid 80s. By this evening and into the overnight \nhours, surface ridging builds in from the north helping to calm \nwinds a bit. With mostly clear skies, efficient radiative cooling \nshould take place with some patchy ground fog becoming possible \naround sunrise Sunday morning. Low-level moisture seems to be fairly \nshallow, so not expecting widespread dense fog, mainly just fog in \nlow-lying areas and river valleys. Expect another nice summer day \nSunday with temperatures topping out in the mid to upper 80s. \nHumidity should again be pleasant by the afternoon as Tds remain in \nthe low to mid 60s.\n\nTemperatures are expected to slowly climb through the week as mid-\nlevel heights increase beginning Monday. High temperatures Monday \nthrough Thursday will be around average for this time of year - \ntopping out in the upper 80s and low 90s. As for rain/storm chances, \nMonday and Tuesday should remain mostly dry as better chances remain \nwell north of the area. By Tuesday night and Wednesday, mid-level \ntroughing ejecting off the northern Rockies will help push an \ninverted trough across Kansas and help to increase precipitation \nchances. A brief lull in precipitation chances may come Thursday \nbefore additional mid-level waves return chances by Friday. Cannot \nrule out some stronger storms in this timeframe given the available \nenergy and shear, but at this time, confidence in widespread \nstrong/severe weather remains low.\n\nRain chances will not be zero heading into the weekend, but as mid-\nlevel ridging builds back east into the central Plains, heat and \nhumid conditions will become the main headline again. Given the 10% \nchance or so of PoPs by the weekend, confidence in heat indicies \ngetting above 105 degrees is low at this time, but NBM probabilities \ncurrently sit in the 20-40% range for HI above 100 degrees by Friday \nthrough Sunday. Extended guidance beyond the weekend seems to keep \nzonal/northwesterly flow across the central and northern Plains so \nnot expecting another long-term heatwave at this time.",
